## Interface: 11509
## Title: WarriorKit
## Notes: Ctrl-click raid marking, a Charge button that aims by camera out of combat, a warrior bar loadout, threat-coloured enemy bars, damage healing and threat meters, square class-coloured player and target frames, a square minimap with the addon buttons collected behind one square, a filter for the red text in the middle of the screen, fast looting, trash selling, repairs paid for you, a longer camera, a carried Edit Mode layout and stripped bar art.
## Author: baudin
## IconTexture: Interface\AddOns\WarriorKit\Media\Icon.tga
## Version: 1.9
## SavedVariables: WarriorKitDB
## SavedVariablesPerCharacter: WarriorKitCharDB
## X-Category: Combat

# The Classic Era TOC, 1.15.9, interface 11509. A client takes Name_<Flavour>.toc
# when one exists and Name.toc otherwise, so this file is what Era loads and
# WarriorKit.toc is what everything else falls back to. Same file list on
# purpose: the differences between the two clients are handled in the code, by
# ns.vanilla and by the probes, not by loading different files.

# Core first. It creates ns.db, the API shims and the registry every feature
# signs into, and the panel walks that registry at PLAYER_LOGIN.
Core\Core.lua

# Two shared files that wrap a client API and own no setting, the same shape
# ns.ItemInfo has. Gear is what the client will let into each hand and which
# number that hand is; Stance is the three stance spells, their localised names
# and which one you are standing in. Both were a part's private knowledge until
# a second part needed the same answer, and both load before anything that asks.
Core\Gear.lua
Core\Stance.lua

# The shared unit layer. What a mob or a group member is, asked once: the
# palette, the level tag, who is in the group and what the threat API says.
# Between Core and UI because none of it draws anything and everything that
# draws a unit wants it. Order inside the folder is dependency order: Color
# before Level, Roster before Threat.
Unit\Unit.lua
Unit\Color.lua
Unit\Level.lua
Unit\Roster.lua
Unit\Threat.lua

# The drawing layer, between Core and everything that draws. It owns the pixel
# grid, so it loads before the panel and before any part that puts a frame up.
UI\Pixel.lua
UI\Draw.lua

# The gauge, between the drawing primitives and anything that puts a bar up. A
# status bar with a flat fill and the spent part of it behind, which the enemy
# bars and the skinned unit frames both draw and both used to draw their own
# way. After UI\Draw.lua because it builds its underlay out of ns.Fill, and
# after Unit\Color.lua because the spent part is a fifth of the fill's colour
# and that fraction belongs to the unit palette.
UI\Gauge.lua

UI\Text.lua
UI\Flow.lua

# One ability, drawn: the icon, the cooldown swipe, the timer, the count and
# the border that carries the status. After UI\Draw.lua for the cropped icon
# and UI\Text.lua for the shared fonts, and before every part that puts a
# castable square on the screen.
UI\Ability.lua

# The widget library, on top of that grid. Theme is the palette and the pixel
# metrics, Stack lays a column out and asks every row how tall it is, Scroll
# clips and scrolls, Widgets is the kit a page is built from, and Window is the
# chrome with its rail and its tab strip. All five load before Core\Panel.lua,
# which is the first thing that builds a page out of them.
UI\Theme.lua
UI\Stack.lua
UI\Scroll.lua
UI\Widgets.lua
UI\Window.lua

Core\Command.lua
Core\Panel.lua

# The parts. Within each, behaviour first and Feature.lua last, because
# Feature.lua is the only file that names anything outside its own folder.
# Perf first among the parts, because Charge/Icon.lua and the three other
# tickers call ns.Perf.Start by name. They resolve it at tick time rather than
# at load, so the order is belt and braces, and belt and braces is what a nil
# namespace on a ticker is worth: that is 1,189 errors in two minutes.
Perf\Perf.lua
Perf\Feature.lua

Marking\Marking.lua
Marking\Keys.lua
Marking\Feature.lua

Charge\Charge.lua
Charge\Icon.lua
Charge\Marker.lua
Charge\SoftTarget.lua
Charge\Feature.lua

Targeting\Switch.lua
Targeting\Feature.lua

Loadouts\Loadouts.lua
Loadouts\Feature.lua

Buttons\Slot.lua
Buttons\Layout.lua
Buttons\Ranks.lua

# The bars themselves, after the three files they read and before Feature.lua
# as everywhere else. Slot.lua says what one action slot is doing, Layout.lua
# resolves which slots a bar drives, and Bars.lua is what draws them, so it
# loads last of the three. It also needs UI\Ability.lua, UI\Flow.lua and the
# pixel grid, all of which are far above this line.
# Hiding the client's own action buttons, and putting them back. Its own file
# because it is the one part of the bars that touches a frame this addon does
# not own, and because how you hide a secure button without taking its taint
# with you is a subject rather than a detail. Before Bars.lua, which calls it.
Buttons\Blizzard.lua

# The plan of which bars this client can have, and the answer to which of them
# you want cloned. Before Bars.lua, which reads both at build time. Its own file
# because the answer stopped being a reading of the client the moment there was
# a tick box per bar, and because the plan is the one part of the bars that is
# source code rather than a setting.
Buttons\Which.lua
Buttons\Bars.lua

# What one square answers to the mouse: the tooltip you get by hovering it and
# the drag that fills it. Split out of Bars.lua at the 800 line gate, and a real
# seam rather than a convenient one: everything in it hangs a script on a button
# and touches the cursor, and none of it knows what a bar is. After Bars.lua,
# which is the only caller, and after Layout.lua, whose probe it asks before it
# picks anything up.
Buttons\Square.lua

# Where each cloned bar sits and the handle you drag it by. After Bars.lua,
# which is the only caller, and separate from it because the bar's shape is
# decided by the plan in source and its position is the one thing you cannot
# sensibly type.
Buttons\Placing.lua
Buttons\Feature.lua

UnitFrames\Plates.lua
UnitFrames\EnemyBars.lua
UnitFrames\Skin.lua
UnitFrames\Feature.lua

# The meters. A damage and healing readout and a threat readout, two panes in
# one frame with no window round them. Who is in the group used to be the first
# file here and is Unit\Roster.lua now, because the enemy bars wanted the same
# answer and were walking the raid on their own ticker to get it. Spec before
# Window, which is the only file that draws an icon. Meter and Threat are
# independent of each other: one reads the combat log and one samples the
# client's threat API, and neither knows the other exists. Feature.lua last, as
# everywhere else.
Meter\Spec.lua
Meter\Meter.lua
Meter\Threat.lua
Meter\Window.lua
Meter\Feature.lua

Artwork\Artwork.lua
Artwork\Feature.lua

# The minimap. Shape.lua makes the frame square and as wide as you asked for,
# Corral.lua holds the other addons' buttons behind one square, and Feature.lua
# last as everywhere else. Shape first because the corral's default position is
# a corner of the map and the map has to have been resized before that corner
# means anything. Next to Artwork because it is the same job on another of
# Blizzard's frames: furniture off, everything reversible in one call.
Minimap\Shape.lua
Minimap\Clock.lua
Minimap\Corral.lua
Minimap\Feature.lua

# The chores. Nothing here is on the combat path, and the first four draw
# nothing at all, so they are independent of each other and of load order within
# the part. Clutter decides what is finished with and Destroy is the window that
# acts on it, so Clutter loads first. Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
#
# Vendor and Repair both wake on MERCHANT_SHOW and share nothing else: one
# empties the bags and one pays for the mending, and neither knows the other
# exists. Vendor is listed first because it is the older of the two and for no
# other reason.
Comfort\Loot.lua
Comfort\Vendor.lua
Comfort\Repair.lua
Comfort\Camera.lua

# The error filter. Not a chore like the four above it, in that nothing runs
# for you; it is a list of messages you have decided you do not need, and one
# replaced method standing in front of the frame that would draw them. Here
# because Comfort is the part that owns the settings that belong to no feature,
# and because the list is one tab rather than a rail entry of its own.
Comfort\Errors.lua
Comfort\Clutter.lua
Comfort\Destroy.lua
Comfort\Feature.lua

# Saved.lua is generated by bake-ui.sh and only assigns a table, so it loads
# after the module that owns the table and before the part that reads it.
EditMode\EditMode.lua
EditMode\Saved.lua
EditMode\Feature.lua

# The addon's own settings, which belong to no feature. Last, because the only
# one of them today is how big a window is drawn and it is applied at
# ADDON_LOADED, by which point every file in this list has run. Behaviour first
# and Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
Settings\Settings.lua
Settings\Feature.lua
